From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Maicon Alves <alvesmaiconn@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 02:05:52 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 51/51] mainmenu: the handheld's world list as Minecraft's
 Select World

The handheld's Start Game tab was a text list of the worlds' names and modes. It is laid out as
Minecraft's Select World now: a row a world, with its picture (icon.png, which the game takes when
the world is left, else the game's icon), its name, when it was last played and its seed, and its
mode, difficulty and type; the latest played on top, the picked one framed. A row picks its world,
a second click on it within half a second plays it, and the list scrolls (the right stick) past
four worlds. Under it are Minecraft's buttons: Play Selected World and Create New World, then
Rename, Delete, Re-create and Exit. Minecraft's Edit is Rename here (world_name in world.mt, the
folder stays), and its Back is Exit, since this list is the first page.

The tab is taller on a handheld (9.9 instead of 7.1) to show four worlds: with one game there is no
game bar, so the tab view keeps no room for it and sits low on the screen, the header above it, its
bottom as far from the edge as its sides at 640x480. The menu writes last_played to world.mt when a world is played or made. pt_BR string
added.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/builtin/common/filterlist.lua b/builtin/common/filterlist.lua
index 12d42c33c..3c2b17c2d 100644
--- a/builtin/common/filterlist.lua
+++ b/builtin/common/filterlist.lua
@@ -268,3 +268,17 @@ function sort_worlds_alphabetic(self)
 		return a.name:lower() < b.name:lower()
 	end)
 end
+
+-- the latest played first (the handheld writes last_played to world.mt), then by name
+function sort_worlds_last_played(self)
+	local played = {}
+	for _, world in ipairs(self.m_processed_list) do
+		played[world] = tonumber(Settings(world.path .. DIR_DELIM .. "world.mt"):get("last_played")) or 0
+	end
+	table.sort(self.m_processed_list, function(a, b)
+		if played[a] ~= played[b] then
+			return played[a] > played[b]
+		end
+		return a.name:lower() < b.name:lower()
+	end)
+end
